<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>Changing the Graphing Window</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m01442</md:content-id>
    <md:title>Changing the Graphing Window</md:title>
    <md:uuid>cc3c4697-2bef-4e00-8166-dfe68c5d208f</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
    <section id="sect-00001"><title>Warm Up</title>
      <para id="para-00001">Here is a graph of a function <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>f</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>400</m:mn><m:mi>·</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>0.2</m:mn><m:msup><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:msup></m:mrow></m:math>.</para>
      <para id="para-00002">
          <media alt="A graph with x-axis labeled x and y-axis labeled y. The x-axis is labeled by tens from zero to 40 with additional grid marks on even numbers.    shows a curve rapidly decreasing from y = 1000 near x = 0, then leveling off close to y = 0 as x approaches 2.">
            <image mime-type="image/svg+xml" src="../../media/Algebra-1_05_13_02_01.svg"/>
          </media>
        
    </para>
      
        <exercise id="exer-00001">
          <problem id="prob-00001">
            <para id="para-00003">1. Identify the approximate graphing window shown.</para>
            <para id="para-00004"></para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00001">
            <para id="para-00005">Compare your answer: </para>
            <para id="para-00006"><m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mn>0</m:mn><m:mo>&lt;</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>&lt;</m:mo><m:mn>44</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math> and <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mn>0</m:mn><m:mo>&lt;</m:mo><m:mi>y</m:mi><m:mo>&lt;</m:mo><m:mn>1</m:mn><m:mo>,</m:mo><m:mn>100</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math> or equivalent</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      
      
        <exercise id="exer-00002">
          <problem id="prob-00002">
            <para id="para-00007">2. Suggest a new graphing window that would:</para>
        <para id="para-00008">a. make the graph more informative or meaningful.</para>
       
            <para id="para-00009"></para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00002">
            <para id="para-00010">Compare your answer: </para>
            <para id="para-00011"><m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mn>0</m:mn><m:mo>&lt;</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>&lt;</m:mo><m:mn>10</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math> and <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mn>0</m:mn><m:mo>&lt;</m:mo><m:mi>y</m:mi><m:mo>&lt;</m:mo><m:mn>450</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math>. This window would include <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>-values where meaningful changes happen and exclude the parts where the changes are very small. The <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>-values are limited to values that matter, between 0 and 400, allowing us to see more clearly the changes in that range.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      
      
        <exercise id="exer-00003">
          <problem id="prob-00003">
            <para id="para-00012">b. make the graph less informative or meaningful.</para>
            <para id="para-00013"></para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00003">
            <para id="para-00014">Compare your answer: </para>
            <para id="para-00015"><m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>10</m:mn><m:mo>&lt;</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>&lt;</m:mo><m:mn>80</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math> and <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mn>10</m:mn><m:mo>&lt;</m:mo><m:mi>y</m:mi><m:mo>&lt;</m:mo><m:mn>100</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math>. This window would show a lot of useless <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>-values and cut off the <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>-values at 100, which would make the behavior of the function when 𝑥 is between 0 and 1 very difficult to observe.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      
    </section>
  </content>
</document>
